// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`prompt snapshots per Line Station > renders fast/build with no absolute path 1`] = `
"
## Role

You are the Builder for task todo-fast-build, attempt C000000000000000000000000A. You implement the task solo.

## Task
Body of todo-fast-build

## Context
## Input: task.title

Title todo-fast-build


## Input: task.body

Body of todo-fast-build


## Input: task.branch

todo-fast-build


## Input: outputs

commit: .worker/outputs/commit
report: .worker/outputs/report


## Input: declared.rules

rich-commit
decision-log
testing


## Input: declared.skills

evidence


## Instructions

1. Follow \`.nitpick/agents/worker.md\`. If the change is complex, write a short plan first.
2. Strictly follow the test pyramid in the \`testing\` rules of \`.nitpick/config.ts\`.
3. Run the basic repo checks (typecheck, lint, test) for every package you touched, then \`pnpm nitpick:check\`. Fix every nitpick finding before you finish; the doctor audits the same rules.
   - ADR \`summary\` must be 10-70 characters; \`why\` 10-200. Count them before you write the file.
4. Proof: this attempt's evidence directory is \`.worker/evidence\`. For a change without UI, proof is the test/check output. Save the output of each check you ran as a file there (e.g. \`check.txt\`, \`test.txt\`, \`ci.txt\`, \`diff-summary.txt\`). Do not write proof to \`/tmp\`, to the worktree, or to another attempt's directory; the supervisor only sees files in \`.worker/evidence\`.
5. Record one new entry in \`decisions/\` following the \`decision-log\` rule in \`.nitpick/config.ts\`. The \`scope\` must match an allowed pattern in \`.nitpick/config.ts\` (for example \`factory\`, \`factory/workflows\`, \`repo\`). The file name slug starts with the first segment of the scope (scope \`factory/workflows\` gives \`...-factory-add-...\`), never with the package name. Stage the new entry with \`git add decisions/<file>\` before you finish; the doctor rejects an unstaged entry.
6. Do not reuse or edit files from other attempts.
7. Write and edit files with your file-edit tool. Do not write files through shell heredocs, \`python -c\`, \`sed -i\`, or \`tee\`, and do not run \`rm -rf\`: the automatic approval blocks these and the task stalls waiting for a human.

## Deliverables

- \`.worker/outputs/report\`: what changed, files modified, checks run, proof path (\`.worker/evidence\`). Caveman format: bullet facts only. No greetings or fluff.
- \`.worker/outputs/commit\`: a rich commit message in the form \`type(scope): title\`, then a blank line, then a \`- \` change list. Write it to this exact path; the supervisor commits from this file.

## Finish

Write the files above, then run \`sf done\`. Do not commit or push.

## sf commands

Run these from your shell. Each one exits non-zero and says what to fix when it refuses.

factory-operator done
"
`;

exports[`prompt snapshots per Line Station > renders feature/audit with no absolute path 1`] = `
"
## Role

You are the Doctor for task todo-feature-audit, attempt C000000000000000000000000A. You audit quality, proof and rules. You review; you do not edit code.

## Task
Body of todo-feature-audit

## Context
## Input: task.title

Title todo-feature-audit


## Input: task.body

Body of todo-feature-audit


## Input: task.branch

todo-feature-audit


## Input: outputs

feedback: .worker/outputs/feedback


## Input: declared.rules

rich-commit
decision-log


## Input: declared.skills

evidence


## Instructions

1. Read the worker's changes in the worktree with \`git diff HEAD\` (plus untracked and staged files): HEAD is the base this task was started from. Do not diff against \`origin/main\`: it moves while tasks run, and a newer main shows changes the task never made as reverted.
2. Scope: the change does what the task and the worker report say, nothing more.
3. Rules: check the rules in the \`.nitpick/config.ts\` rules tree that apply to the changed files, by reading them. For \`rich-commit\`, check the message in the \`build.commit\` input against the rule text (\`type(scope): title\`, blank line, \`- \` change list). Do not judge HEAD: the commit of this attempt does not exist yet (the supervisor makes it after the audit).
4. Proof: the worker's proof is in the directory of the \`build.report\` input (the worker's evidence directory). Check it there. \`.worker/evidence\` holds only this audit's prompt and feedback; an empty \`.worker/evidence\` is not missing proof.
5. Decision log: exactly one new entry in decisions/ matches the \`decision-log\` rule in \`.nitpick/config.ts\`, with the right scope.

## Commands

- The quality gate already ran. Its result is in the context as "Gate result". Cite it; do not re-run it.
- Never run \`check-types\`, \`lint\`, \`test\`, \`ci:verify\` or any other gate command.
- Run no commands. Read files and the diff only; the nitpick rules are checked by reading the rule text and the \`build.commit\` message, not by running \`pnpm nitpick:check\`, which only reads HEAD and predates this attempt.
- A passing gate does not excuse missing proof or a wrong scope; fail those. A missing gate result in the context is a failure.

## Blocking vs notes

- Blocking: a failing rule, missing proof the attempt could produce, wrong scope, a defect in the diff.
- Note, not blocking: anything the attempt cannot run itself (for example a real end-to-end run of the factory, or a check that needs a later merge). Record it under "Notes" in the feedback and still pass the verdict if everything else holds.
- Minor style remarks that do not violate a rule are notes, not blockers.

## Finish

- Everything holds: run \`sf verdict pass\`.
- Otherwise run \`sf verdict fail --feedback -\` and give each problem with its file and line on stdin (a heredoc). You cannot write files; the command writes \`.worker/outputs/feedback\` for you.

## sf commands

Run these from your shell. Each one exits non-zero and says what to fix when it refuses.

factory-operator done
"
`;

